-- chem/pauli.cub — Dirac to Schrödinger as a Schur complement, and where Foldy and Wouthuysen's operator sits, tested
-- as a program in one dimension (operators in x and D = d/dx acting on polynomials over the rationals; p = −iD, so
-- p² = −D², p⁴ = D⁴, pXp = −DXD, and no i appears). Dirac's equations for the large and small components, E from the
-- rest energy: Vφ + pχ = Eφ, pφ + (V − 2m)χ = Eχ. Eliminating χ = (2m + E − V)⁻¹pφ is the Schur complement onto φ,
-- exact: [V + p(2m + E − V)⁻¹p]φ = Eφ. With μ = 1/2m and ε = μ² = 1/4m² (ε tags the order 1/c²), the resolvent is
-- μ − ε(E − V) with the exact remainder (1 + μw)(1 − μw) = 1 − εw², w = E − V (`pl-res`), so to first order in ε the
-- Schur complement is S(E) = V − μD² + εD(E − V)D: Hermitian, but depending on E. Since it is linear in E,
--   S(E) − E = A − E·B,   A = V − μD² − εDVD,   B = 1 − εD²   (`pl-pencil`):
-- the Schur complement to this order is a linear pencil (A, B), B positive (1 + εp²), the metric of the large
-- component (the small one carries ‖χ‖² = ε‖pφ‖²). Its standard form is the congruence by C = B^{−1/2} = 1 + (ε/2)D²
-- (the renormalization φ → (1 + p²/8m²)φ): the coefficient of ε in C·B·C is 0 (`pl-metric`) and in C·A·C is
--   −DVD + ½(D²A₀ + A₀D²) = ½V″ − μD⁴,   A₀ = V − μD²   (`pl-fw`),
-- that is C·A·C = p²/2m + V − p⁴/8m³ + V″/8m² + O(ε²): Foldy and Wouthuysen's operator in one dimension (kinetic
-- correction and Darwin's term; the spin–orbit term needs σ in three dimensions and is not in this model). The
-- identity under it is D²V + VD² − 2DVD = V″: on V = x^k and f = x^n both sides are (a polynomial in n and k of degree
-- at most 2 in each)·x^{n+k−2}, namely (n+k)(n+k−1) + n(n−1) − 2n(n+k−1) = k(k−1), so agreement at k, n ∈ {0 … 4}
-- proves it for every k and n, and by linearity in V and in f for every polynomial V and f; μ, ε and E enter each
-- identity with degree at most 1, so two values of each suffice (`pl-grid`: the 8 combinations of {1/2, 1/3}).
-- So: no unitary of the Dirac operator is built, but Foldy and Wouthuysen's operator is not the Schur complement's
-- expansion itself; it is the standard form of the expansion's pencil under the congruence by B^{−1/2}. For counts
-- of levels this is the collapse stack's statement twice: by Haynsworth, the levels of the Dirac matrix below E are
-- the positron block's (those of V − 2m − E, all of them at chemical E) plus those of the Schur complement at E; and
-- the levels of A − E·B are those of C(A − E·B)C = H_FW − E + O(ε²) by congruence (Sylvester). Stated here, proved
-- in the collapse stack (chem/linalg.cub and after), not in this module.
import "laplace.hypercubical"

